  "description": "A 1-minute de-risking session on Mental Health and the Adolescent Brain.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "Three out of four lifetime mental health conditions begin before age 24. It is a time of vulnerability.",
      "visual": "🧩"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "The adolescent brain is undergoing massive synaptic pruning and myelination, remodeling circuits for emotion, reward, and social.",
      "visual": "🏗️"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "This remodeling creates windows of fragility.",
      "visual": "⚠️"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "Schizophrenia often emerges in late adolescence because excessive pruning in the prefrontal cortex strips away too many synapses.",
      "visual": "✂️"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "Early intervention during adolescence can redirect brain development.",
      "visual": "🌱"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "The adolescent brain is not fragile because it is flawed.",
      "visual": "🔧"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "Why do many mental health conditions first appear during adolescence?",
    "options": [
      "Ongoing brain remodeling creates windows of vulnerability",
      "The brain stops producing neurotransmitters entirely",
      "Adolescents have fewer neurons than children"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
